NEARS-3639 a11y finding (review-cycle QA point, ux-1 re-check): Order-updates /
Offers-promotions rows still expose TWO accessibility nodes at identical bounds,
not one.

Source: docs/qa-evidence/NEARS-3639/settings-page-a11y-dump.xml (live uiautomator
dump, emulator-5556, English locale, Settings screen).

Extract for the "Order updates" row (bounds [96,1001][1248,1193]):

<node class="android.widget.Button" content-desc="Order updates, switch"
      focusable="true" clickable="true" bounds="[96,1001][1248,1193]">
  <node class="android.view.View" content-desc="" focusable="true" clickable="false"
        bounds="[96,1001][1248,1193]">
    <node class="android.widget.Switch" content-desc="Order updates"
          checkable="true" checked="true" clickable="true" focusable="true"
          bounds="[96,1001][1248,1193]" />
  </node>
</node>

Same shape for "Offers & promotions" (bounds [96,1194][1248,1386]).

Root cause (packages/nears_dls/lib/elements/settings_tile/n_settings_tile.dart):
the row wraps itself in `Semantics(container: true, explicitChildNodes: true,
label: semanticLabel, ...)`. Per this repo's own active review lesson
`dls-semantics-missing-container-boundary`: "only a configuration with
explicitChildNodes:false can absorb another [SemanticsNode]" -- i.e.
explicitChildNodes:true structurally PREVENTS the child NSwitch from merging
into the parent's node. The child Switch therefore remains its own,
separately-focusable AccessibilityNodeInfo, alongside the parent Button node
that carries the intended "Order updates, switch" label. On Android this is
the standard "row Button wraps its own focusable Switch" a11y anti-pattern:
without excluding the inner Switch from the a11y tree (or exposing
checked-state on the OUTER node instead and excluding the switch), TalkBack's
linear (swipe) navigation is expected to stop on BOTH nodes -- once on the row
as a generic labeled button, once on the nested Switch with its on/off state
-- which is the doubled announcement ux-1 set out to fix, not a single control.

What I could NOT establish with 100% certainty: an actual TalkBack speech
transcript proving 2 stops (vs 1). ADB's synthetic `input tap` bypasses
TalkBack's touch-exploration gesture layer and instead ACTIVATES the control
directly (verified live -- a tap while TalkBack was bound opened the
notification_confirm_sheet immediately, see run notes), so a live "swipe
next/previous" traversal count could not be captured with this toolset in
this session. The structural evidence above (2 distinct focusable
AccessibilityNodeInfo objects at identical bounds, plus the codebase's own
documented explicitChildNodes contract) is the live-device signal this QA
pass could obtain.

Suggested fix shape (standard Android pattern, matches this DLS's own
NIconButton/NActiveOrderBanner precedents): either (a) wrap the trailing
NSwitch in ExcludeSemantics and expose `toggled:`/`checked` on the OUTER
Semantics wrapper instead, so exactly one node carries both the label and the
state, or (b) drop explicitChildNodes and re-derive a merged label without
relying on Flutter's implicit Text-node concatenation (which is what
explicitChildNodes was added to avoid).
